# Week 3 review: openings and plans
teaches: ["openings/italian-game","openings/black-against-e4","openings/black-against-d4","strategy/planning"]
voices: {"a":"gemini:Charon | calm, warm chess coach","b":"gemini:Puck | eager club player, relaxed and good-humoured; never exaggerated"}
prompt: "Can you play your three openings from memory? Can you find the plan in a quiet position?"
next: {"title":"Can the king catch the pawn?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/chess-30-days/23-can-the-king-catch-the-pawn.cast"}

## Warm-up: queen grab, pawn fork
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: rn1qkbnr/ppp2p1p/3p2p1/4N3/2B1P3/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BbK2R w KQkq - 0 6, moves: [Bxf7+, Ke7, Nd5#]}
      - {fen: r1bq1rk1/ppp2ppp/2nb1n2/8/2BPP3/5N2/1P3PPP/RNBQ1RK1 w - - 0 1, moves: [d5, Ne5]}
      - {fen: r1bq1rk1/ppp2ppp/2nb1n2/8/2BPP3/5N2/1P3PPP/RNBQ1RK1 w - - 0 1, moves: [e5]}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0

    card title Warm-up

A: Week three, reviewed: can you play your openings from memory, and find a plan when nothing is happening? Two warm-ups first.
    draw position

B: Black just took my queen on d1. That's the game over.
    highlight piece_d1

A: Look at his king, not your queen. Checks first.
    highlight piece_e8

    pause 0.3

    ask "Mate in two." widget chess line true hints ["Checks first: which piece can give check?","Your bishop on c4 can take on f7."] themes ["trap","mating pattern"] voice a teaches ["openings/traps","tactics/mating-patterns"]
        right: Bishop takes f7, check. The king's only square is e7, and knight to d5 is mate. It's called Légal's mate.
        wrong: Forget the queen. Which check leaves his king almost no squares?

B: Three small pieces beat a queen, because they all pointed at his king.
    highlight piece_e5 piece_d5 piece_f7

A: Second one, from an Italian.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: Your pawns stand side by side on d4 and e4.
    highlight piece_d4 piece_e4

B: I push d5 and hit the knight on c6.
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.3

A: It just steps away to e5, and now it hits your bishop. Nothing won.
    animate plies_shown 2 duration 1.3

A: Take it back, and look again.
    animate position_shown 2 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win a piece." widget chess line true hints ["A pawn can attack two pieces at once.","Your pawn on e4."] themes ["fork","pawn structure"] voice a teaches ["tactics/fork","strategy/pawn-structure"]
        right: Pawn to e5 hits the bishop and the knight at once. Black can save only one of them.
        wrong: Which pawn move attacks two black pieces on the same rank?

B: So the big centre is a weapon, not just a wall.
    highlight piece_e5

A: Both came out of openings. Today: can you play yours from memory, and what comes after the book?
    highlight piece_d4 piece_e5

## Memory, then the plan
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1, moves: [e4, e5, Nf3, Nc6, Bc4, Bc5, c3, Nf6, d4, exd4, cxd4, Bb4+, Bd2, Bxd2+, Nbxd2], flip: true}
      - {fen: r1bqk2r/pppp1ppp/2n2n2/8/2BPP3/5N2/PP1N1PPP/R2QK2R b KQkq - 0 8, moves: [O-O, e5], flip: true}
      - {fen: r1bqk2r/pppp1ppp/2n2n2/8/2BPP3/5N2/PP1N1PPP/R2QK2R b KQkq - 0 8, moves: [d5, exd5, Nxd5], flip: true}
      - {fen: 2r2rk1/1pqnbppp/p2p4/4p3/4P3/1BN5/PPP1QPPP/R4RK1 w - - 0 1, moves: [h3, Nb6]}
      - {fen: 2r2rk1/1pqnbppp/p2p4/4p3/4P3/1BN5/PPP1QPPP/R4RK1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Nd5, Qd8], highlights: [d6, d5], arrows: [{from: a1, to: d1}]}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0
    safety: 0

    card title "Memory, then the plan"

A: First question: your openings, from memory. Here's your Black against the Italian, from Black's side.
    draw position

B: Pawn to e5, knight to c6, bishop to c5. I know this bit.
    blocking false

    animate plies_shown 2 duration 1

    animate plies_shown 4 duration 1

    animate plies_shown 6 duration 1

A: White plays c3, your knight hits e4, and d4 arrives. You take, White takes back, and your bishop checks.
    blocking false

    animate plies_shown 7 duration 0.9

    animate plies_shown 8 duration 0.9

    animate plies_shown 9 duration 0.9

    animate plies_shown 10 duration 0.9

    animate plies_shown 11 duration 0.9

    animate plies_shown 12 duration 0.9

B: The bishops swap off, and I've run out of moves I memorised.
    blocking false

    animate plies_shown 14 duration 1.2

    animate plies_shown 15 duration 1

A: Then ask what the moves were for. White's two centre pawns want to roll forward.
    highlight piece_d4 piece_e4

    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.2

B: So I castle, like the rules say.
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.3

A: And pawn to e5 kicks your knight off its best square.
    animate plies_shown 2 duration 1.3

    highlight piece_f6 duration 1.2

A: Take that back. Your move: which move stops White's pawns rolling forward?
    animate position_shown 2 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    quiz "Which move stops White's pawns rolling forward?" on_canvas true buttons_at.x 905 buttons_at.y 375 buttons_layout column voice a
        right: Pawn to d5 hits e4 at once. If White pushes on to e5, your knight jumps to e4, so the pawns can't roll.
        wrong: Look for the move that hits e4 before it can move.
        * O-O
        + …d5
        * …h6

A: Pawn to d5. White takes, and your knight takes back, right in the centre.
    blocking false

    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.2

    animate plies_shown 2 duration 1.2

    animate plies_shown 3 duration 1.2

B: So I don't need move nine memorised. I need to know what move eight was for.
    highlight piece_d5

A: Second question: a quiet position. Back to White's side of the board.
    animate position_shown 3 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: Nothing hangs, and no check or capture works, for either side.
    animate safety 1 duration 1

    animate safety 0 duration 0.5

B: Then I make a safe move. Pawn to h3.
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.3

A: Safe, but it does nothing. Black uses the time: knight to b6, guarding d5.
    animate plies_shown 2 duration 1.3

    animate position_shown 4 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    draw hl_d6

A: So ask the pawns instead. Black's pawn on d6 has no pawn left to guard it.
    highlight piece_d6

A: Which square is a hole, where no black pawn can ever attack?

    quiz "Which square is a hole, where no black pawn can ever attack?" on_canvas true buttons_at.x 905 buttons_at.y 375 buttons_layout column voice a
        right: d5: Black's c-pawn is gone and his e-pawn has passed it, so nothing can chase a white piece away from there.
        wrong: For each square, find a black pawn that could step up and attack it.
        + d5
        * f5
        * b5

    draw hl_d5

A: So the knight goes to d5 now, before Black's knight can guard it.
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.3

B: It hits his queen, and she steps back.
    animate plies_shown 2 duration 1.3

A: And his bishop on e7 can never touch it. It moves on dark squares, stuck behind its own pawns.
    highlight piece_e7

A: If his knight comes to f6 to swap it off, your bishop on b3 takes back on d5.
    highlight piece_b3 piece_d7

A: Then a rook comes to the d-file, lined up for when the knight steps aside.
    draw arrow_0

A: That's a plan: a target, and your pieces moving toward it.
    highlight piece_d6 piece_d5 piece_a1

## Your turn: the week-three test
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: Three for three. Your openings have teeth.
    good: Most of them. The miss comes back until it sticks.
    poor: Traps are learned by falling in once. The misses come back.
    none: The test will wait for you.
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: r1bqkb1r/pppn1ppp/5n2/3N2B1/3P4/8/PP2PPPP/R2QKBNR b KQkq - 0 6, moves: [Nxd5, Bxd8, Bb4+], flip: true}
      - {fen: r1b1kbnr/pppp1Npp/8/6q1/2BnP3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQK2R b KQkq - 0 5, moves: [Qxg2], flip: true}
      - {fen: r4rk1/p1q1bppp/1p3n2/8/6P1/1N2B2Q/PPP2P2/1K1R3R w - - 0 1, moves: [g5]}
      - {fen: rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1}
    position_shown: 3
    plies_shown: 0
    openings:
      - {name: 'Giuoco Piano, with d4', eco: C54, side: white, moves: [e4, e5, Nf3, Nc6, Bc4, Bc5, c3, Nf6, d4], idea: 'c3 came first, so a pawn can take back on d4: a big centre.'}
      - {name: 'Giuoco Pianissimo, with d3', eco: C54, side: white, moves: [e4, e5, Nf3, Nc6, Bc4, Bc5, c3, Nf6, d3], idea: 'A pawn guards e4; castle first, and push d4 later.'}
      - {name: Giuoco Piano with c3, eco: C53, side: black, moves: [e4, e5, Nf3, Nc6, Bc4, Bc5, c3, Nf6], idea: 'The knight hits e4 at once, and c3 has taken its defender''s square.'}
      - {name: Scotch Game, eco: C45, side: black, moves: [e4, e5, Nf3, Nc6, d4, exd4, Nxd4, Bc5], idea: 'Take the pawn, then hit the knight on d4 a second time.'}
      - {name: Queen's Gambit Declined, eco: D35, side: black, moves: [d4, d5, c4, e6, Nc3, Nf6], idea: 'e6 holds d5: if White takes there, a pawn takes back.'}
      - {name: London System, side: black, moves: [d4, d5, Bf4, Nf6, e3, c5], idea: 'c5 hits d4, and b2 lost its guard when the bishop left c1.'}

    card title "Your turn"

A: Now your turn: three positions from this week's openings, no labels, and a score at the end.
    draw position

A: Your Queen's Gambit Declined, so the board turns to Black's side.
    animate position_shown 0 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: White's knight has just taken on d5.
    highlight piece_d5

    pause 0.3

    ask "Find the best move." widget chess line true hints ["Is your knight on f6 really pinned to anything that matters?","Take on d5, then look at the check on b4."] themes ["trap","opening"] voice a test week3 teaches ["openings/traps","openings/italian-game","openings/black-against-e4","openings/black-against-d4"]
        right: Knight takes d5. The bishop takes your queen, and bishop to b4 is check. White's only block is the queen; you take her, the kings take back, and you're a piece up for a pawn: the Elephant Trap.
        wrong: Count what happens if White's bishop takes your queen. Is there a check after that?

A: Black again. White's knight has just taken on f7,
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: forking your queen and rook.
    highlight piece_g5 piece_h8

    pause 0.3

    ask "Find the best move." widget chess line true hints ["Your queen has a target of her own.","The pawn on g2."] themes ["trap"] voice a test week3 teaches ["openings/traps"]
        right: Queen takes g2 and hits the rook on h1. If it runs to f1, queen takes e4, check; if the bishop blocks, knight to f3 is mate. That's the Blackburne–Shilling trap.
        wrong: Don't save the rook. What can your queen take, with a threat?

A: Last one: back to White, with the kings castled on opposite sides.
    animate position_shown 2 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Find the best move." widget chess line true hints ["What holds h7?","A pawn can chase it away."] themes ["king attack"] voice a test week3 teaches ["strategy/king-attack"]
        right: Pawn to g5 hits the knight, the only piece holding h7. With the rook behind the queen, the king's guard doesn't help: wherever the knight goes, h7 or the knight itself falls.
        wrong: Your queen and rook already aim at h7. What stands in their way?

    hide position

    text t_1 "Elephant Trap" x 500 y 600 font_size 40
    text t_2 "Shilling trap" x 500 y 540 font_size 40
    text t_3 "Pawn storm" x 500 y 480 font_size 40
    parallel true

A: You scored {week3.score} of {week3.total}.
    results test week3

    erase t_1 t_2 t_3

B: And the ones I missed?
    text review "Review" x 500 y 440 font_size 72
    text review_sub "your missed puzzles" x 500 y 360 font_size 36

A: They come back now, with any you missed in earlier weeks.
    explore activity review_misses

    erase review review_sub

    animate position_shown 3 plies_shown 0 duration 0.1

    show position

A: Now the openings themselves: the Italian as White, and both your defences as Black.
    highlight piece_e2 piece_d7

A: Play each one from memory. The board turns to face your side.
    explore activity openings_drill

    hide position

A: Openings and plans ask one question: what is this move for?
    text w_q "What is it for?" x 500 y 450 font_size 72

A: Know the job, and the next move is already there.
    text w_job "job → move" x 500 y 340 font_size 48

    erase w_q w_job

A: Homework: one slow game today, fifteen plus ten or slower. Then find its first mistake.
    text hw_t "Homework" x 500 y 520 font_size 56
    text hw "15 + 10" x 500 y 420 font_size 72
    text hw_sub "first mistake?" x 500 y 320 font_size 40

## Where next
end_page: true

    text end_next_kicker "Next" x 500 y 540 font_size 26 style.opacity 0.6
    link end_next href lecture:23 title "Can the king catch the pawn?" form title size 600 x 500 y 420

    link end_again href lecture:22 title "Watch again" form text mark replay open here x 500 y 250 font_size 28
    text end_count "22 of 30" x 500 y 180 font_size 22 style.opacity 0.6
